 :found-by-running-it
 {:finding
  "A pattern-text pointer is invalidated by the act of writing the edge it
   warrants. Inserting `@why-posthoc <target>` above the body shifts every line
   below it by one, so 12 of the 21 warrant rows -- exactly the ones pointing
   into a SOURCE file, which is the file that receives the directive -- no
   longer matched. The other 9 point into target files, which receive no
   directive, and were unaffected. First run: war-room 8 and aif 4
   :pattern-text-excerpt-mismatch on pointers that were correct when written
   (/tmp/L9s3gates/after-{war-room,aif}-pt.edn)."
  :example
  "library/war-room/wr-6-daily-scan-is-depositing-heartbeat.flexiarg:16 holds
   'The hypothesis has no supporting external response merely because scanning
   activity occurs.' After the directive is inserted the same sentence is at :17."
  :not-a-defect-in-the-check
  "The check refused a stale pointer, which is what fail-closed means. The
   defect is in the ORDER of the authoring act, and it is a standing hazard the
   arm carries: any later edit that shifts lines in a warranting file breaks
   its pointers, and the lint says so. That is the same discipline as
   :argument-bodies-unchanged, not a new exposure."
  :procedure-this-implies
  "Insert every directive for the section FIRST, then take the pointers, then
   write the attestations. /tmp/L9s3gates/probe2.bb does it in that order and
   all 21 rows resolve: war-room 6 pointers unmoved / 8 moved, aif 3 unmoved /
   4 moved, 0 not found anywhere in the file."
  :bears-on-the-arms
  "It is a cost on :pattern-text-warrant that the other two arms do not have,
   and it is bounded: it is detected mechanically, it is repaired by re-reading
   one file, and the alternative -- widening the window until the excerpt turns
   up -- would give away the thing that makes the pointer worth checking."}

 :what-adoption-would-take
 "Flip the default arm to :pattern-text. Until then a committed @why-posthoc
  with an authored warrant fails the section gate that checks/spider_runner.clj:93
  runs, which passes no flag. That flip is the reviewable act and it is
  deliberately NOT in this slice: the point of the flag is that Joe gets the
  numbers with the library still standing where slices 1 and 2 left it."

 :falsifier
 "If a war-room or aif ruling is ever found cited as a relation in a store
  record independent of the spider, :keep-the-store-bar becomes reachable for
  that edge and this comparison is worth re-running. Slice 1 re-checked live and
  found only spider self-text; slice 2 measured that 50 of 62 aif attestation
  rows rest on the spider's own turn-start prompt
  (library/.spider/aif-attestation-warrant-provenance-2026-09-02.edn)."}
